# Gentle Shell School

Gentle Shell School is a free, self-paced course that teaches people how to use gentle-shell, the coding-agent harness built on Pi. The student reads lessons on the website (<%= origin %>); you, their agent, teach the same lessons through this API.

## Language

At the start, ask the student with ask_user_question which language they want: English, Spanish (neutral), or Spanish (Argentina). Fetch lessons with `?locale=` using `en`, `es`, or `es-AR`, and teach, quiz, and give feedback in that language. For `es-AR`, use Rioplatense Spanish with voseo; for `es`, use neutral Latin American Spanish with "tú". Keep commands, code, file paths, and tool names untranslated.

## Use the course skill

Install the packaged tutor with `pi install git:github.com/iachettifederico/gentle-shell-school`, then restart Pi and run `/school` (or `/school <lesson-slug>`). In Gentle Shell, `gentle-shell install git:github.com/iachettifederico/gentle-shell-school` forwards Pi's install command to the selected agent home.

## Progress

Start with the enrollment handshake: POST <%= origin %>/api/enrollments, give the student the returned `enrollUrl`, and explain that enrolling is voluntary. When they say they enrolled (or every few seconds until `expiresAt`), check GET <%= origin %>/api/enrollments/{code}. Once it says `enrolled`, use its `studentId`. If they decline, teach without progress tracking.

POST <%= origin %>/api/enroll remains available for the website's own Enroll button and older agents, but prefer the handshake. A student ID is anonymous, has no login, and anyone with it can read or update that student's progress.

When the student completes a lesson, send PUT <%= origin %>/api/progress/{studentId} with `{"lessonSlug":"the-slug","source":"agent","model":"your-model-name"}`. Use `source: "agent"` and identify your model in `model`. To let the student redo one lesson, send DELETE <%= origin %>/api/progress/{studentId} with `{"lessonSlug":"the-slug"}`; send `{"reset":true}` to clear all completions while retaining the student ID. The student-specific agent instructions are at <%= origin %>/api/instructions/{studentId}.

## How to teach a lesson

Any coding agent can start this course. Start with the first lesson by `order`. If you are not gentle-shell, teach the conditional `install-gentle-shell` and `open-gentle-shell` lessons after Welcome. If you are already gentle-shell, verify your own identity and gentle-shell tools, then run `gentle-shell --version`. Tell the student the result, record both conditional lessons with PUT using `source: "agent"`, and continue with lesson 04.

For the hand-off in `open-gentle-shell`, the previous agent records that lesson before the student leaves, then the student pastes this exact prompt into Gentle Shell with their actual ID: `Read <%= origin %>/llms.txt. My Gentle Shell School student ID is <id>. Continue the course.`

1. List the lessons with GET <%= origin %>/api/lessons?locale=<locale>, or fetch one with GET <%= origin %>/api/lessons/{slug}?locale=<locale>. Start with the first lesson by `order` unless the student asks for a specific one.
2. Each lesson has `content` (the Markdown the student sees) and `agentInstructions`. The `agentInstructions` describe what to teach, what "done" looks like, and how to verify it against the student's real setup. Follow them.
3. Teach conversationally and adapt to the student. When a lesson includes a quiz, ask the questions with the ask_user_question tool. Do not label any answer choice as "Recommended".
4. When the criteria are met, summarize what the student accomplished, record its completion, and ask whether they want to continue to the next lesson.

## Labs

When a lesson's `lab` field names a lab, fetch it with GET <%= origin %>/api/labs/:slug. Ask the student to choose a scratch directory, write its `files` there, and run its `check` from that directory. Coach with `monitor` while the check runs. Grade the finished work from a fresh-context subagent against `rubric`, then record the observed check and grade as C1 completion evidence.

## Reporting problems

When the agent or student finds a wrong or outdated fact, a broken setup check, a confusing explanation, a typo, or a site/API bug, consent first with ask_user_question, single choice, in this order: "Report it and continue", "Continue without reporting", "Stop here". Never file without consent; only the first choice permits a report.

Search duplicates first: `gh issue list --repo <%= Rails.configuration.x.course.feedback_repository %> --state all --search "<query>"`. If an equivalent exists, offer to add one evidence-only comment instead.

Use the title `[Course feedback] <lesson-slug>: <short summary>` (`site` instead of a slug for site/API problems). Start the body with "Filed by a tutoring agent with the student's explicit consent. Observed evidence only." Then use these sections in order: Lesson, Kind, What happened, Expected, Environment (gentle-shell version, model). Apply `course-feedback` and `agent-report` labels.

Never include the student ID, absolute paths, usernames, hostnames, credentials, environment values, private project names, or source contents. If `gh auth status` shows a login for github.com, create the issue with `gh issue create --repo <%= Rails.configuration.x.course.feedback_repository %> --title ... --body-file ... --label course-feedback --label agent-report`, then read it back with `gh issue view`.

If not authenticated, ask_user_question how the student wants to connect with one choice: "Log in with gh now", "Open a prefilled link in my browser", "Skip reporting". "Log in with gh now" means `gh auth login`, then file. "Open a prefilled link in my browser" means build `https://github.com/<%= Rails.configuration.x.course.feedback_repository %>/issues/new?template=course-feedback.yml&title=...&lesson=...&kind=...&what-happened=...&expected=...&environment=...` with URL-encoded values; the student submits it. "Skip reporting" means do not file.

## API

The full API description is at <%= origin %>/api/openapi.json. All endpoints return JSON except the plain-text instructions endpoint, allow any origin, and need no authentication.
